Build HFS into the kernel, so the RAM disk can be mounted as root.

The kernel reaches the root mount with a working memory device and cannot do
anything with it:

    BSD root: md0, major 2, minor 0
    bsd_init: vfs_mountroot
    cannot mount root, errno = 19

ENODEV, because no filesystem in this tree claimed the device. xnu-4903 has
no HFS: Apple moved it out after 10.12 and bsd/vfs/vfs_conf.c does not mention
it anywhere. IOFindBSDRoot() asks for Apple_HFS by default, so HFS is both
what the kernel wants and what a ramdisk image can plausibly be.

The sources are staged by the build script from hfs-407.30.1 -- the 10.14-line
tag that still ships core/. Two later tags of the same line, hfs-407.50.6 and
hfs-407.200.4, carry only the userland tools.

Apple's directory layout is kept: core/ and hfs_encodings/ are staged as
siblings under bsd/hfs, because sources in each reach into the other with
relative includes -- core/hfs.h includes "../hfs_encodings/hfs_encodings.h"
and hfs_encodings/hfs_encodinghint.c includes "../core/hfs.h". Flattening
them into one directory breaks both.

Of hfs_encodings, only hfs_encodinghint.c is built. It is needed because
hfs_catalog.c calls hfs_pickencoding() from a branch guarded by
!TARGET_OS_EMBEDDED rather than by CONFIG_HFS_STD, and nothing defines
TARGET_OS_EMBEDDED here. The cost is nil: with CONFIG_HFS_STD=0 the 800 lines
of CJK encoding tables compile away and what is left is a stub returning
kTextEncodingMacRoman, plus the encoding-bias accessors. hfs_encodings.c is
a different kext (HFSEncodings, with its own start/stop in
hfs_encodings_kext.cpp) and is not built: everything it defines --
hfs_getconverter, hfs_relconverter, the MacRoman converters -- is reached only
through the standard-HFS path, which is off.

The third relative include, "../tests/hfs_extents_test.h" in hfs_extents.c,
sits inside #if HFS_EXTENTS_TEST and never fires, so tests/ is not staged.

Registration does not go through vfs_conf.c, which is why its silence does not
matter. The kext registers itself:

    struct vfs_fsentry vfe = {
            .vfe_vfsops = &hfs_vfsops,
            .vfe_fsname = "hfs",
            .vfe_flags  = ... | VFS_TBLCANMOUNTROOT | ...
    };
    int ret = vfs_fsadd(&vfe, &vfs_handle);

VFS_TBLCANMOUNTROOT is already there, so nothing has to be added for HFS to be
a root filesystem. What has to change is who calls it. That code sits in
com_apple_filesystems_hfs::start(), an IOService method, and a filesystem
linked into the kernel has no IOService to be started. So hfs_iokit.cpp loses
the class, the metaclass registration and the stop() method, start() becomes

    extern "C" int hfs_module_start(void)

and bsd_init() calls it directly, after vnode_pager_bootstrap() and before the
root mount goes looking for a filesystem. The one member the class held,
vfs_handle, becomes a file static. The rest of hfs_iokit.cpp stays: it also
holds hfs_is_ejectable(), hfs_iterate_media_with_content() and
hfs_get_platform_serial_number(), which the filesystem calls.

Nothing is renamed. All 40 sources have names no existing xnu object uses --
checked against bsd, osfmk, iokit and libkern -- so unlike the pthread kext
there is no collision to work around.

The configuration is given as -D flags rather than through core/kext-config.h.
That file is a prefix header (GCC_PREFIX_HEADER in core/kext.xcconfig) that no
source includes, and it keys the whole configuration off TARGET_OS_EMBEDDED.
What is defined here is deliberately small: CONFIG_HFS_STD=0, because nothing
needs to read 1980s HFS, and HFS_COMPRESSION=1, because the kernel has decmpfs
(config_fs_compression is in this configuration's FILESYS_BASE) and an iOS
system volume is full of compressed files. FIFO, QUOTA, NAMEDSTREAMS,
CONFIG_SEARCHFS and CONFIG_HFS_DIRLINK are left undefined, which the sources
read as off. They can be turned on one at a time once a volume mounts at all.

CONFIG_PROTECT is the one that has to be switched off rather than left out,
with -UCONFIG_PROTECT. Leaving it out is not enough: config_protect is in this
configuration's FILESYS_BASE, so config/MASTER defines it for the whole
kernel, and the HFS sources pick it up. Both C_RULE_1A and P_RULE_1A put
${$@_CFLAGS_ADD} after ${CFLAGS}, so the -U wins over the config's -D -- the
same mechanism the pthread kext uses to get the KPI view of the headers.

Content protection has two reasons not to be on. It cannot work: every key
operation ends in a callPlatformFunction to AppleKeyStore, which is not part
of this kernel. And the published sources do not compile with it on --
hfs_readwrite.c calls cp_handle_vnop(), cp_io_params(), cp_vnode_transcode()
and cp_handle_strategy() without including hfs_cprotect.h, the only header
that declares them, and hfs_key_roll.c reads cp_key_roll_ctx out of struct
cprotect, where that member exists only under HFS_CONFIG_KEY_ROLL. Off, both
hfs_cprotect.c and hfs_key_roll.c become empty translation units, which is
what their end-to-end #if CONFIG_PROTECT is for, and every cp_* call site in
hfs_readwrite.c disappears with them.

One AppleKeyStore reference survives being switched off, and that is the
second thing this patch corrects. core/hfs_iokit.h includes
<AppleKeyStore/AppleKeyStoreFSServices.h> unconditionally, and hfs_vfsops.c
and hfs_vfsutils.c include that header unconditionally for three functions
that have nothing to do with keys. The include and the four key declarations
that need it are given the CONFIG_PROTECT guard that their only caller,
hfs_cprotect.c, already has; the matching definitions at the end of
hfs_iokit.cpp -- key_services() and the four wrappers -- get the same guard.

The third correction is sysctlbyname(). hfs_journal.c calls it to size the
transaction buffer from hw.memsize, and that call compiles in a kext because
config/Libkern.exports maps _sysctlbyname to _kernel_sysctlbyname. Inside the
kernel the name belongs to the syscall entry point, which takes
(proc_t, struct sysctlbyname_args *, int32_t *), and bsd/sys/sysctl.h declares
the five-argument form only in its !KERNEL branch. So the real function is
declared and the name redirected to it where def_tbuffer_size is defined.

The fourth is ubc_create_upl(), the same shape as sysctlbyname one layer down.
config/BSDKernel.exports maps _ubc_create_upl to _ubc_create_upl_external, and
bsd/sys/ubc.h declares the plain name only inside #ifndef XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE
-- the kext view. Everything with that define gets ubc_create_upl_external()
instead, which has the identical signature and only supplies the VM tag with
vm_tag_bt(), so hfs_readwrite.c redirects the name to it.

Those four are the whole set, not a sample. Two sweeps over this tree say so:
of every function declared only in the kext view of xnu's bsd headers,
ubc_create_upl is the one HFS calls; and of the 49 _public:_real aliases in
config/*.exports, HFS calls three -- sysctlbyname, ubc_create_upl and kalloc,
and kalloc is a working macro inside the kernel.

How the flags are applied matters, and the one C++ source makes it awkward
twice over. hfs_iokit.cpp is the first and only .cpp in bsd/conf/files, and
two mechanisms here are C-only.

The first is add_perfile_cflags, in makedefs/MakeInc.def:

    define add_perfile_cflags
    $(1)_CWARNFLAGS_ADD += $2
    endef

Only C_RULE_1A reads _CWARNFLAGS_ADD; P_RULE_1A reads _CXXWARNFLAGS_ADD. So
anything passed that way reaches the 39 C files and silently misses the C++
one. The defines therefore go through _CFLAGS_ADD, which both rules read, and
the warning suppressions are applied once to the C objects through
add_perfile_cflags and once to the C++ object through _CXXWARNFLAGS_ADD.

The second is the object's name. The conf tool builds it by replacing the
last character of the source file name with 'o' (SETUP/config/mkmakefile.c:
cp = sp + strlen(sp) - 1; *cp = 'o'), so a .c becomes .o and a .cpp becomes
.cpo. The object is hfs_iokit.cpo, which is what iokit/conf/Makefile.template
calls its own C++ objects as well. Naming it hfs_iokit.o sets variables that
no rule ever reads, which is exactly how -UCONFIG_PROTECT came to be present
in the makefile and absent from the command line that compiled hfs_iokit.cpp.

One more thing follows from linking a kext in rather than loading it, and it
is the same shape as the duplicate pthread_kern: hfs_sysctl_register() must
not be called. bsd/sys/sysctl.h emits the __sysctl_set linker-set entry only

    #ifdef XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE
    #define SYSCTL_LINKER_SET_ENTRY LINKER_SET_ENTRY
    #else
    #define SYSCTL_LINKER_SET_ENTRY(a, b)
    #endif

A kext is compiled without that define, so its oids never reach the set, and
that is exactly why HFS chains them onto a list of its own and registers them
by hand from start(). These sources, compiled in the bsd component, do have
XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE: the entries land in __DATA,__sysctl_set and
sysctl_early_init() registers all of them at bsd_init.c:421, hundreds of lines
before this function runs. Doing it again hands sysctl_register_oid() an oid
that is already in its parent's list, and the ordered SLIST_INSERT_AFTER makes
that element's next pointer refer to itself; the list is circular from then on
and the next walk never returns. The call is removed, the oids still work.

hfs_module_start() names each of its stages on the console, the way bsd_init
traces its own. It is called from inside bsd_init rather than loaded as a kext
at leisure, so anything in it that does not finish stops the boot before the
root mount with nothing printed between "calling hfs_module_start" and
silence. The four lines -- module start, vfs_fsadd, zones, sysctls, registered
-- cost nothing and say which stage was reached.

This gets a filesystem into the kernel. It does not yet get a volume into the
ramdisk: the image the shim carries still has to be made, and made as HFS+.

--- a/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
+++ b/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
@@ -150,6 +150,62 @@
 
 $(foreach file,$(OBJS_NO_SIGN_COMPARE),$(eval $(call add_perfile_cflags,$(file),-Wno-sign-compare)))
 
+# HFS is staged into bsd/hfs by the build script rather than living in this
+# tree, so it carries its own include path. The configuration comes from here
+# rather than from core/kext-config.h, which is a prefix header this build
+# does not use and which would key CONFIG_PROTECT off TARGET_OS_EMBEDDED --
+# turning on AppleKeyStore key wrapping that does not exist here.
+#
+# CONFIG_PROTECT has to be switched off rather than merely left out: this
+# configuration's FILESYS_BASE carries config_protect, so config/MASTER turns
+# it on for the whole kernel. Content protection cannot work here in any case
+# -- every key operation ends in an AppleKeyStore platform function -- and the
+# published sources do not even compile with it on: hfs_readwrite.c uses
+# cp_handle_vnop(), cp_io_params() and friends without including the header
+# that declares them, hfs_cprotect.h. Both C_RULE_1A and P_RULE_1A put
+# ${$@_CFLAGS_ADD} after ${CFLAGS}, so the -U wins over the config's -D.
+#
+# Everything else not named below is left undefined, which the sources read
+# as off.
+#
+# Note hfs_iokit.cpo. The conf tool names an object by replacing only the
+# last character of the source file name, so a .cpp becomes .cpo -- the same
+# convention iokit/conf/Makefile.template uses. It is the one C++ source here
+# and the only one in bsd/conf/files.
+HFS_OBJS =			\
+		BTree.o	BTreeAllocate.o	BTreeMiscOps.o \
+		BTreeNodeOps.o	BTreeNodeReserve.o	BTreeScanner.o \
+		BTreeTreeOps.o	BTreeWrapper.o	CatalogUtilities.o \
+		FileExtentMapping.o	FileIDsServices.o	MacOSStubs.o \
+		UnicodeWrappers.o	VolumeAllocation.o	hfs_attrlist.o \
+		hfs_btreeio.o	hfs_catalog.o	hfs_chash.o \
+		hfs_cnode.o	hfs_cprotect.o	hfs_endian.o \
+		hfs_extents.o	hfs_fsinfo.o	hfs_hotfiles.o \
+		hfs_iokit.cpo	hfs_journal.o	hfs_key_roll.o \
+		hfs_link.o	hfs_lookup.o	hfs_notification.o \
+		hfs_quota.o	hfs_readwrite.o	hfs_resize.o \
+		hfs_search.o	hfs_vfsops.o	hfs_vfsutils.o \
+		hfs_vnops.o	hfs_xattr.o	rangelist.o \
+		hfs_encodinghint.o
+
+HFS_DEFINES = -I$(SRCROOT)/bsd/hfs/core -DCONFIG_HFS_STD=0 -DHFS_COMPRESSION=1 \
+              -UCONFIG_PROTECT
+
+HFS_WARNFLAGS = -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes \
+                -Wno-sign-compare -Wno-cast-align -Wno-shorten-64-to-32
+
+# The defines go through _CFLAGS_ADD, which both C_RULE_1A and P_RULE_1A read.
+# add_perfile_cflags cannot carry them: it appends to _CWARNFLAGS_ADD, which
+# only the C rule reads, so the C++ object would silently get none of them.
+define add_hfs_defines
+$(1)_CFLAGS_ADD += $(2)
+endef
+
+$(foreach file,$(HFS_OBJS),$(eval $(call add_hfs_defines,$(file),$(HFS_DEFINES))))
+$(foreach file,$(filter-out hfs_iokit.cpo,$(HFS_OBJS)),$(eval $(call add_perfile_cflags,$(file),$(HFS_WARNFLAGS))))
+
+hfs_iokit.cpo_CXXWARNFLAGS_ADD += $(HFS_WARNFLAGS)
+
 # Objects that don't want -Wcast-align warning (8474835)
 OBJS_NO_CAST_ALIGN =			\
 		audit_bsm_token.o	\
